lesson "The Dot Product" {
  course: "pure-maths"
  unit: "Vectors"
  difficulty: "Advanced"
  icon: "Link"
  summary: "Multiply two vectors and get a plain number. It measures how much they agree, which makes it the fastest test for perpendicular there is."
  skills: ["vec-dot-product", "vec-perpendicular", "vec-angle"]

  slide "A Product That Is Not a Vector" {
    id: "v4-hook"
    cat: "Two Formulas"
    > The **dot product** (or scalar product) of two vectors is a single number, not
    > a vector. That is why it is called scalar, and it catches people out
    > constantly.
    >
    > There are two formulas and they are equal:
    >
    > $$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = a_1b_1 + a_2b_2$$
    > $$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = |\mathbf{a}||\mathbf{b}|\cos\theta$$
    >
    > The first is what you compute with. The second is what it **means**: the
    > product of the two lengths, scaled by how well aligned they are.
    >
    > That $\cos\theta$ is the whole story. Pointing the same way gives $\cos 0 = 1$
    > and the biggest possible value. At right angles $\cos 90° = 0$ and the product
    > vanishes. Pointing oppositely gives $\cos 180° = -1$ and it goes negative.
    >
    > Swing the second vector, and stretch it too. Both dials feed the same number.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6, 6]
      y: [-5, 5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param th = 45 { range: [0, 180], step: 5 }
      param bl = 3 { range: [0.5, 4], step: 0.1 }
      vector a = (0, 0) -> (4, 0) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      vector b = (0, 0) -> (bl*cos(rad(th)), bl*sin(rad(th))) { color: accent, width: 4 }
      arc ang = (0, 0) { r: 1.2, from: 0, to: th, color: neutral, width: 3 }
      label at (-5.7, 4.4) = "|a| = 4  ·  |b| = ${bl}" { color: neutral }
      label at (-5.7, 3.6) = "angle ${th}°" { color: neutral }
      label at (-5.7, 2.8) = "a · b = ${4*bl*cos(rad(th))}" { color: primary }
      label at (-5.7, 2) = "they agree" { color: success, show: cos(rad(th)) > 0.02 }
      label at (-5.7, 2) = "perpendicular: zero" { color: warning, show: abs(cos(rad(th))) <= 0.02 }
      label at (-5.7, 2) = "they oppose" { color: danger, show: cos(rad(th)) < 0 - 0.02 }
      slider th { label: "angle between the vectors" }
      slider bl { label: "length of b" }
    }

    goal "Find the angle that makes the dot product exactly zero" {
      when: abs(cos(rad(th))) < 0.02
      hint: "Perpendicular. A right angle means no agreement at all."
    }

    goal "Now make the dot product zero a completely different way" {
      when: bl < 0.55
      hint: "If one of the vectors has no length, there is nothing to agree about."
    }
  }

  slide "The Shadow One Vector Casts on the Other" {
    id: "v4-projection"
    cat: "Two Formulas"
    > Here is the picture that makes $|\mathbf{a}||\mathbf{b}|\cos\theta$ stop being a
    > formula and start being obvious.
    >
    > Shine a light straight down onto $\mathbf{a}$. The **shadow** $\mathbf{b}$ casts
    > has length $|\mathbf{b}|\cos\theta$ — that is just the adjacent side of the right
    > triangle. So
    >
    > $$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = |\mathbf{a}| \times (\text{the shadow of } \mathbf{b} \text{ on } \mathbf{a})$$
    >
    > The dot product asks **how much of b goes a's way**, and then multiplies by how
    > long $\mathbf{a}$ is. That is all it has ever asked.
    >
    > Swing $\mathbf{b}$ up towards the vertical and watch the shadow shrink to
    > nothing. Push past that and it reappears on the *other side*, which is exactly
    > where the minus sign comes from.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6.5, 7]
      y: [-3, 5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param th = 50 { range: [0, 180], step: 1 }
      param bl = 3.5 { range: [1, 4.5], step: 0.1 }
      vector a = (0, 0) -> (5, 0) { color: primary, width: 8, opacity: 0.25 }
      line shadow = (0, 0) -> (bl*cos(rad(th)), 0) { color: success, width: 8 }
      line drop = (bl*cos(rad(th)), bl*sin(rad(th))) -> (bl*cos(rad(th)), 0) { color: neutral, style: dashed, width: 2 }
      vector b = (0, 0) -> (bl*cos(rad(th)), bl*sin(rad(th))) { color: accent, width: 4 }
      arc ang = (0, 0) { r: 1, from: 0, to: th, color: neutral, width: 2 }
      label at (-6.4, 4.4) = "shadow = |b| cos ${th}° = ${bl*cos(rad(th))}" { color: success }
      label at (-6.4, 3.6) = "a · b = 5 × ${bl*cos(rad(th))} = ${5*bl*cos(rad(th))}" { color: primary }
      label at (-6.4, -2.4) = "the shadow has flipped to the far side, so the product is negative" { color: danger, show: cos(rad(th)) < 0 - 0.02 }
      slider th { label: "swing b" }
      slider bl { label: "length of b" }
    }

    goal "Swing b past the right angle until its shadow falls on the wrong side" {
      when: cos(rad(th)) < 0 - 0.05
      hint: "Past 90°. The shadow shrinks to zero first, then reappears pointing backwards."
    }
  }

  slide "Both Formulas, One Number" {
    id: "v4-two-formulas"
    cat: "Two Formulas"
    > The claim was that $a_1b_1 + a_2b_2$ and $|\mathbf{a}||\mathbf{b}|\cos\theta$ are
    > the same number. Worth checking rather than believing.
    >
    > Drag either arrowhead. The top line multiplies components and adds; the bottom
    > line multiplies lengths and scales by the angle. They never disagree.

    scene plane {
      x: [-6, 6]
      y: [-5, 5.5]
      aspect: equal
      grid
      axes
      param ax = 4 { range: [-5, 5], step: 1 }
      param ay = 1 { range: [-4, 4], step: 1 }
      param bx = 1 { range: [-5, 5], step: 1 }
      param by = 3 { range: [-4, 4], step: 1 }
      vector a = (0, 0) -> (ax, ay) { color: primary, width: 4 }
      vector b = (0, 0) -> (bx, by) { color: accent, width: 4 }
      point ta = (ax, ay) { drag: xy -> (ax, ay), color: primary, r: 8, snap: 1 }
      point tb = (bx, by) { drag: xy -> (bx, by), color: accent, r: 8, snap: 1 }
      label at (-5.9, -2.6) = "${ax}×${bx} + ${ay}×${by} = ${ax*bx + ay*by}" { color: primary }
      label at (-5.9, -3.5) = "|a| = ${hypot(ax, ay)}  ·  |b| = ${hypot(bx, by)}" { color: accent }
      label at (-5.9, -4.4) = "× cos ${deg(acos((ax*bx + ay*by)/(hypot(ax, ay)*hypot(bx, by))))}° = ${ax*bx + ay*by}" { color: accent, show: hypot(ax, ay) > 0.5 and hypot(bx, by) > 0.5 }
      label at (-5.9, -4.4) = "give both arrows a length" { color: neutral, show: hypot(ax, ay) <= 0.5 or hypot(bx, by) <= 0.5 }
      label at (-5.9, 4.8) = "perpendicular" { color: warning, show: ax*bx + ay*by == 0 and hypot(ax, ay) > 0.5 and hypot(bx, by) > 0.5 }
    }

    goal "Make the dot product exactly zero, with both vectors still having a length" {
      when: ax*bx + ay*by == 0 and hypot(ax, ay) > 0.5 and hypot(bx, by) > 0.5
      hint: "Swap the two components of one vector and negate one of them. 4 and 1 becomes -1 and 4."
    }
  }

  slide "The Perpendicular Test" {
    id: "v4-perpendicular"
    cat: "Right Angles"
    > That zero is the single most useful fact about the dot product:
    >
    > $$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = 0 \iff \mathbf{a} \text{ and } \mathbf{b} \text{ are perpendicular}$$
    >
    > (assuming neither is the zero vector). Testing for a right angle becomes one
    > multiplication and one addition, with no angles or trigonometry anywhere.
    >
    > Check $3\mathbf{i} + 4\mathbf{j}$ against $4\mathbf{i} - 3\mathbf{j}$:
    >
    > $$3(4) + 4(-3) = 12 - 12 = 0$$
    >
    > Perpendicular. And notice how those components were built: swap them and
    > negate one — exactly the "flip and change the sign" rule for perpendicular
    > gradients. Same geometry, different notation.

    numeric {
      ask "What is $(2\mathbf{i} + 5\mathbf{j}) \cdot (10\mathbf{i} - 4\mathbf{j})$?"
      skill: "vec-dot-product"
      answer: 0
      expect: 2*10 + 5*(0 - 4)
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$2 \times 10 + 5 \times (-4)$."
      onwrong: "v4-not-componentwise" retry
      ! "$20 - 20 = 0$, so those two are perpendicular. The dot product found a right angle without measuring one."
    }
  }

  slide "Multiply Across, Then Add" {
    id: "v4-not-componentwise"
    hidden: true
    cat: "Right Angles"
    > The dot product is **not** done component by component. It does not give a
    > vector at all.
    >
    > $$(2\mathbf{i} + 5\mathbf{j}) \cdot (10\mathbf{i} - 4\mathbf{j}) = \underbrace{2 \times 10}_{20} + \underbrace{5 \times (-4)}_{-20} = 0$$
    >
    > Multiply the matching components, **then add the results into one number**.
    > There is no $\mathbf{i}$ or $\mathbf{j}$ left in the answer.
    >
    > Compare it with addition, which really is componentwise and really does give a
    > vector:
    >
    > $$(2\mathbf{i} + 5\mathbf{j}) + (10\mathbf{i} - 4\mathbf{j}) = 12\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j}$$
    >
    > Adding keeps the components apart. The dot product deliberately collapses them
    > together, and that collapse is what lets a single number describe the angle.

    numeric {
      ask "What is $(3\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{j}) \cdot (2\mathbf{i} + 6\mathbf{j})$?"
      skill: "vec-dot-product"
      answer: 0
      expect: 3*2 + (0 - 1)*6
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "$3 \times 2 + (-1) \times 6$, and add the two results."
      ! "$6 - 6 = 0$. Perpendicular again, and again the answer is a single number rather than a vector."
    }
  }

  slide "Finding the Angle" {
    id: "v4-angle"
    cat: "Right Angles"
    > Setting the two formulas equal gives you the angle between any two vectors:
    >
    > $$\cos\theta = \frac{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}}{|\mathbf{a}||\mathbf{b}|}$$
    >
    > Compute the dot product, divide by both magnitudes, take the inverse cosine.
    >
    > For $\mathbf{a} = 3\mathbf{i} + 4\mathbf{j}$ and $\mathbf{b} = 5\mathbf{i}$:
    > the dot product is $15$, the magnitudes are $5$ and $5$, so
    > $\cos\theta = 15/25 = 0.6$ and $\theta \approx 53.1°$.
    >
    > A useful check: that fraction can never leave the range $-1$ to $1$. If yours
    > does, the arithmetic went wrong somewhere.

    numeric {
      ask "For $\mathbf{a} = 3\mathbf{i} + 4\mathbf{j}$ and $\mathbf{b} = 5\mathbf{i}$, find the angle between them in degrees."
      skill: "vec-angle"
      answer: 53.13
      expect: deg(acos(15/25))
      tolerance: 0.3
      hint "$\cos\theta = 15/(5 \times 5) = 0.6$."
      ! "$\arccos(0.6) \approx 53.1°$. That is the $3,4,5$ triangle's angle, which is exactly what $\mathbf{a}$ describes."
    }
  }

  slide "Sort by Angle" {
    id: "v4-sort"
    cat: "Reading the Sign"
    > You can read the rough geometry off the **sign** alone, without computing an
    > angle at all.

    sort {
      ask "Sort each dot product by what it says about the angle between the vectors."
      skill: "vec-dot-product"
      bin "Acute, under 90°": ["$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = 12$", "$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = 0.5$"]
      bin "Exactly 90°": ["$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = 0$"]
      bin "Obtuse, over 90°": ["$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = -7$", "$\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b} = -30$"]
      hint "The sign of the dot product is the sign of $\cos\theta$, since the magnitudes are always positive."
      ! "Positive means they broadly agree, zero means perpendicular, negative means they broadly oppose. The *size* depends on the lengths too, but the sign is purely about the angle."
    }
  }

  slide "Everything Together" {
    id: "v4-synth"
    cat: "Reading the Sign"
    numeric {
      ask "Find the value of $k$ that makes $2\mathbf{i} + k\mathbf{j}$ perpendicular to $6\mathbf{i} - 3\mathbf{j}$."
      skill: "vec-perpendicular"
      answer: 4
      expect: 12/3
      tolerance: 0.01
      hint "Perpendicular means the dot product is zero: $2(6) + k(-3) = 0$."
      ! "$12 - 3k = 0$, so $k = 4$. Turning a geometric condition into a one-line equation is what the dot product is best at."
    }
  }
}
